    "narrative": "The Austin Waves enter today\u2019s slate with **Kyle Tucker** (79.40) standing as our premier offensive anchor. Facing Trevor Rogers in a matchup bolstered by favorable wind conditions, **Kyle Tucker** is primed for a high-ceiling performance. Alongside him, **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.** (72.88) provides elite run production potential against Rea, benefitting from a solid SP skill matchup that elevates his base projection. Meanwhile, **Andy Pages** (71.04) rounds out the top tier of our lineup; his score is significantly buoyed by a slow opposing pitcher delivery\u2014a key situational factor that increases his high-value stolen base probability\u2014and additional wind assistance.\n\nWhile our starting nine is set, the decision to bench **Miguel Vargas** (61.26) was our tightest call of the morning. **Miguel Vargas** produced an efficient score that would warrant a start on most days, but he is squeezed out by the sheer depth of our mid-tier producers like **Jacob Wilson** (68.49) and **Gunnar Henderson** (65.41). Both **Jacob Wilson** and **Gunnar Henderson** offer higher floor probabilities against their respective right-handed matchups, with **Gunnar Henderson** specifically drawing value from an elite hold projection despite the stiff challenge posed by Yoshinobu Yamamoto.\n\nWe remain cautious regarding the high-risk, high-reward nature of **Cole Carrigg** (50.00), who faces a daunting assignment against Paul Skenes. While the park factors provide a +14% tailwind for **Cole Carrigg**, the SP skill deficit is substantial, and he is a primary candidate for a mid-game pinch-hit if the matchup stalls. Similarly, owners should monitor the \"Lineup Pending\" status for **Nolan Schanuel** and **Brayan Rocchio**; while both are projected in favorable spots, any late-breaking lineup changes could force a re-evaluation of our efficiency targets to ensure we aren't burning limited games on sub-optimal slots.",
